<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <color name="colorPrimary">#512BD4</color>
    <!-- What Android paints the status bar with, through MAUI's theme. Not a darker shade of the
         primary, despite the Material name: the bar sits directly above Orbit's navigation bar, and the
         web puts the same surface colour there (app.css gives the sidebar its elevated background, and
         below 680px that sidebar becomes the top bar). Left as the template's indigo it read as a band
         of a colour nothing else on the screen uses. Matches SurfaceLight in
         Resources/Styles/Colors.xaml; see values-night for the other half. Only visible up to API 34 -
         from 35 Android draws the app behind the bar instead. -->
    <color name="colorPrimaryDark">#FFFFFF</color>
    <!-- What Android tints its own controls with: the line under a focused text field, a checkbox tick,
         a text selection handle. Orbit's default accent, not the MAUI template's indigo, which was a
         colour the app used nowhere on the one mark that says "you are typing here".

         It cannot follow a reader who picks a different accent: a theme colour is fixed when the
         activity inflates, and the obvious lever for changing it afterwards - tinting the widget -
         paints the field's whole background, because MAUI draws that from the same drawable. Following
         the choice properly means giving inputs a drawable of their own. See App.ApplyAccent for the
         colours everything Orbit draws itself does follow. -->
    <color name="colorAccent">#7260CB</color>
</resources>
